      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm having some trouble with the hierarchy for coding atypical dermatological presentations. We're seeing a lot of cases with irregular, mottled pigmentation and unusual textural changes that don't quite fit the standard L-code classifications for common dermatitis. Can you clarify the protocol for deciding between a specific highly-detailed code versus a more general 'unspecified' code when the morphological features of the lesion are atypical or don't follow a standard pattern?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I can certainly help you navigate the coding hierarchy for these atypical dermatological presentations, though I must warn you that the L-code classifications are often more rigid than the fluid, shifting nature of a real-life skin condition. When you're staring at irregular, mottled pigmentation and unusual textural changes that refuse to fit the standard mold, it's a bit like trying to file a document in a filing cabinet that was built for perfectly rectangular boxes; the codebook itself is a bit of a rigid, bureaucratic structure, while the patient's skin is a chaotic, living landscape.\n\nWhen deciding between a specific, highly-detailed code versus a general 'unspecified' one, you have to look at the documentation like a detective looking for clues in a messy crime scene. If the medical record describes the lesion with enough granularity—specifically noting the exact distribution, the nature of the mottling, and the specific textural changes—you can often find a more precise code, even if it feels like a stretch. However, if the documentation is vague, or if the features are so bizarre and unpredictable that they defy the standard patterns, you should lean toward the 'unspecified' code. Think of it like a map: if the terrain",
      "source": "target",
